WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.677
Welcome.

00:00:00.677 --> 00:00:04.949
This recorded workshop is about getting more from the bird photographs you
already have.

00:00:04.949 --> 00:00:12.805
We will look at how much you can crop, what makes a crop suitable for a
print, why a sharp eye can come with blurred wingtips, and how to choose

00:00:12.805 --> 00:00:13.591
between similar frames.

00:00:13.591 --> 00:00:18.412
The workshop is made by BirdFrame, so there is a short demonstration of the
app near the end.

00:00:18.412 --> 00:00:22.040
Most of the session is about decisions you can use with any photo editor.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.808
The voice is synthetic narration.

00:00:23.808 --> 00:00:30.628
You can pause whenever you want, and the page below the video has the
slides, a transcript and the free tools used in the examples.

00:00:30.628 --> 00:00:36.777
BirdFrame began with a friend who spent hours cropping and looking closely
through thousands of bird photographs after trips.

00:00:36.777 --> 00:00:40.592
Batch cropping made the birds easier to see and the pictures easier to
review.

00:00:40.592 --> 00:00:48.272
That is the practical problem behind this workshop: how do we spend less
time repeating the same steps, while keeping control of the pictures we

00:00:48.272 --> 00:00:48.579
choose?

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:51.773
There are three decisions to keep in mind.

00:00:51.773 --> 00:00:54.923
First, what do you want the finished photograph to show?

00:00:54.923 --> 00:00:58.775
A close portrait, the bird's behaviour, or its place in the habitat?

00:00:58.775 --> 00:01:01.089
Second, where will the picture be used?

00:01:01.089 --> 00:01:06.149
A phone screen, a club projector, a print and a competition can need
different dimensions.

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:09.348
Third, which original frame gives you the best starting point?

00:01:09.348 --> 00:01:13.845
We will start with a picture containing two birds, then work through crop
size and reach.

00:01:13.845 --> 00:01:19.206
After that come movement, selecting a frame from a burst, and a checklist
for competition entries.

00:01:19.206 --> 00:01:23.656
Finally, we will see how batch cropping works and how to try it on Mac or
Windows.

00:01:23.656 --> 00:01:27.625
If you have a recent bird photograph nearby, keep it in mind as we go.

00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:30.637
You do not need to install anything to follow the workshop.

00:01:30.637 --> 00:01:37.694
A useful outcome is simply to leave knowing which crop to make, why it has
enough pixels for its purpose, and which original to preserve.

00:01:38.694 --> 00:01:40.678
Here is an original bird photograph.

00:01:40.678 --> 00:01:42.466
There are two birds in the branches.

00:01:42.466 --> 00:01:45.793
Before looking at the next slide, try finding both of them.

00:01:45.793 --> 00:01:50.463
Think about whether you would keep the two birds together or make a separate
photograph of each one.

00:01:50.463 --> 00:01:52.483
There is no single right composition.

00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:58.020
A wider frame may tell you more about the setting, while a tighter frame can
make the subject easier to see.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.343
Take a few seconds to decide what you want this picture to say.

00:02:01.343 --> 00:02:13.343
Pause and consider your own photograph.

00:02:14.343 --> 00:02:18.520
Here are the same two birds marked in the original, alongside the separate
crops.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.242
The second bird is partly hidden.

00:02:20.242 --> 00:02:26.185
These are real BirdFrame detections and crops from the original photograph,
not invented birds or filled-in detail.

00:02:26.185 --> 00:02:30.640
Notice the distinction between finding the subject and deciding the final
composition.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.902
A tool can give you a useful first crop.

00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:39.927
You still decide whether the branch matters, whether the bird needs more
space in the direction it faces, and whether two birds belong together in

00:02:39.927 --> 00:02:40.489
the story.

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:42.683
That is why it helps to retain the original.

00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:47.894
A close crop is one interpretation of the photograph, not a replacement for
everything outside it.

00:02:47.894 --> 00:02:53.477
If you later want a different shape, a wider view, or two birds together,
you can return to the source.

00:02:53.477 --> 00:02:57.659
For your own picture, ask two questions before moving any crop handles.

00:02:57.659 --> 00:02:58.824
What is the subject?

00:02:58.824 --> 00:03:01.352
And what part of the surroundings helps explain it?

00:03:01.352 --> 00:03:06.585
Removing everything else is often a good starting point, but the
surroundings are not automatically clutter.

00:03:07.585 --> 00:03:09.965
Cropping removes pixels around the subject.

00:03:09.965 --> 00:03:13.690
It does not, by itself, make the pixels you keep less sharp.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:20.566
A small bird that is a little soft in the original will still be a little
soft in the crop; it will simply be easier to see that softness when the

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:21.896
bird fills more of the screen.

00:03:21.896 --> 00:03:24.904
The important habit is to crop from the full-size original.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:28.762
Suppose your original is six thousand pixels wide and four thousand high.

00:03:28.762 --> 00:03:36.642
If you first export a copy that is only two thousand and forty-eight pixels
wide, then crop that copy, you have already thrown away most of the

00:03:36.642 --> 00:03:38.977
available pixels before you start framing the bird.

00:03:38.977 --> 00:03:44.583
Enlarging the finished crop changes its output dimensions but does not
recover the original captured detail.

00:03:44.583 --> 00:03:50.518
Tools that synthesize new detail are a different kind of edit and may not
suit documentary or competition uses.

00:03:50.518 --> 00:03:53.373
Check the rules for the destination before relying on them.

00:03:53.373 --> 00:03:57.512
Also avoid repeatedly opening and saving the same JPEG at a lossy setting.

00:03:57.512 --> 00:04:03.445
Keep the camera original, do your editing from that source, and make a
delivery copy for the place you are sharing it.

00:04:03.445 --> 00:04:07.077
A full-resolution crop means the retained area is not downscaled.

00:04:07.077 --> 00:04:11.754
It does not mean a small crop still contains the original photograph's full
pixel count.

00:04:12.754 --> 00:04:15.796
Print size is a useful calculation rather than a mystery.

00:04:15.796 --> 00:04:19.150
Divide the pixel dimensions by the intended pixels per inch.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:26.045
A crop that is three thousand pixels wide and two thousand high makes a ten
by six point seven inch print at three hundred pixels per inch.

00:04:26.045 --> 00:04:28.021
The table gives several examples.

00:04:28.021 --> 00:04:32.073
These are dimensions, not a guarantee that every file will look equally
sharp.

00:04:32.073 --> 00:04:36.917
Focus, motion, noise, processing, paper and viewing distance still matter.

00:04:36.917 --> 00:04:43.498
A slightly softer photograph can work well as a modest print, while a large
file can disappoint if the bird was out of focus.

00:04:43.498 --> 00:04:45.901
There is an easy trap in the phrase half a crop.

00:04:45.901 --> 00:04:49.360
Halving the width and halving the height retains one quarter of the pixels.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.975
A six thousand by four thousand photograph becomes three thousand by two
thousand: six megapixels rather than twenty-four.

00:04:55.975 --> 00:04:58.530
That is still a useful image for many purposes.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.979
Try this calculation with a two thousand four hundred by three thousand
pixel crop.

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:06.726
At three hundred pixels per inch, what print dimensions do you get?

00:05:06.726 --> 00:05:08.315
Divide each side separately.

00:05:08.315 --> 00:05:10.173
The answer is eight by ten inches.

00:05:10.173 --> 00:05:16.023
If the print lab needs a different shape, allow for that additional crop
before deciding whether the file is large enough.

00:05:17.023 --> 00:05:22.096
This table compares a fourteen-centimetre bird with a four hundred
millimetre lens on several cameras.

00:05:22.096 --> 00:05:27.590
Treat it as a geometric comparison with the same assumptions, not a ranking
of overall camera quality.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:34.757
The numbers describe pixels across the bird, not autofocus, lens sharpness,
low-light quality or the chance of getting the shot.

00:05:34.757 --> 00:05:36.379
Read across one row first.

00:05:36.379 --> 00:05:39.754
As distance increases, the bird occupies fewer pixels.

00:05:39.754 --> 00:05:41.528
Read down one column next.

00:05:41.528 --> 00:05:48.136
Cameras with different pixel densities put different numbers of pixels
across the same subject at the same distance with the same lens.

00:05:48.136 --> 00:05:51.640
A crop mode by itself does not create extra detail on the bird.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.228
It changes the portion of the sensor you retain.

00:05:54.228 --> 00:05:59.620
Likewise, a higher megapixel number does not tell the whole story without
knowing the sensor size.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:03.838
What matters for this comparison is how densely the pixels cover the image
of the bird.

00:06:03.838 --> 00:06:08.636
The green cells mark one illustrative threshold, fifteen hundred pixels
across the bird.

00:06:08.636 --> 00:06:10.738
That is not a universal pass or fail.

00:06:10.738 --> 00:06:14.904
A small web image and a large close-viewed print ask different things of the
photograph.

00:06:14.904 --> 00:06:23.365
Use the reach calculator to compare a specific camera, lens, bird size and
distance, then remember that the real photograph also depends on technique

00:06:23.365 --> 00:06:24.070
and conditions.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.866
If the bird is too small, there are several choices.

00:06:27.866 --> 00:06:31.767
Cropping changes the framing without increasing the captured detail on the
bird.

00:06:31.767 --> 00:06:35.759
A longer lens or a teleconverter can make the bird's image larger on the
sensor.

00:06:35.759 --> 00:06:39.735
Moving closer can do that too, when it is safe and does not disturb the
bird.

00:06:39.735 --> 00:06:46.351
A one point four times teleconverter increases the linear image size by
roughly that factor and loses about one stop of light.

00:06:46.351 --> 00:06:50.946
A two times converter gives roughly twice the linear size and loses about
two stops.

00:06:50.946 --> 00:06:53.294
Compatibility and optical quality matter.

00:06:53.294 --> 00:06:57.329
More pixels across a blurred subject are not the same as more useful detail.

00:06:57.329 --> 00:07:02.405
The table illustrates how moving from twenty metres to ten metres compares
with adding focal length.

00:07:02.405 --> 00:07:06.722
It is a simplified example, not a reason to approach wildlife too closely.

00:07:06.722 --> 00:07:08.386
The bird's welfare comes first.

00:07:08.386 --> 00:07:14.520
Stay back from nests and sensitive behaviour, follow local access rules, and
let the bird choose its distance.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:18.506
Waiting quietly in an appropriate place may be a better option than
advancing.

00:07:18.506 --> 00:07:22.469
Sometimes the best answer is to keep the wider environmental photograph.

00:07:22.469 --> 00:07:25.427
A bird does not need to fill the frame for the picture to work.

00:07:25.427 --> 00:07:33.076
If the habitat, light or behaviour is the story, preserving that context can
be more valuable than forcing a close portrait from too few pixels.

00:07:34.076 --> 00:07:35.745
Now consider a flying bird.

00:07:35.745 --> 00:07:39.893
When you pan with it, you are trying to reduce the bird's movement across
the frame.

00:07:39.893 --> 00:07:42.230
The wings are still moving relative to the body.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.886
That is why a photograph can have a sharp eye and visibly blurred wingtips.

00:07:45.886 --> 00:07:48.351
Wing movement also changes during a wingbeat.

00:07:48.351 --> 00:07:55.164
In a simplified picture of the motion, the wingtips move quickly through the
middle of the stroke and more slowly near the turn at the top and bottom.

00:07:55.164 --> 00:08:01.285
Two pictures from the same burst can therefore show very different wingtip
sharpness even though the camera settings did not change.

00:08:01.285 --> 00:08:07.785
A shutter speed around one two-thousandth of a second is a useful example
starting point for some birds in flight, not a promise.

00:08:07.785 --> 00:08:14.058
Species, distance, direction, magnification and your intended output all
affect how much motion you see.

00:08:14.058 --> 00:08:16.855
Check your own results at the size you plan to use them.

00:08:16.855 --> 00:08:19.281
Decide what you want before judging the picture.

00:08:19.281 --> 00:08:22.796
A little wing blur can communicate movement while the head remains clear.

00:08:22.796 --> 00:08:30.051
If your goal is a fully frozen wing, you may need brighter light, a faster
shutter, a different moment in the wingbeat, or a different framing.

00:08:30.051 --> 00:08:33.778
Sharpness is one part of the photograph, not its entire purpose.

00:08:34.778 --> 00:08:41.733
These examples estimate how fast a shutter would need to be to limit wingtip
movement to a few pixels under a particular set of assumptions.

00:08:41.733 --> 00:08:46.565
The swallow example is far faster than the maximum mechanical shutter speed
of many cameras.

00:08:46.565 --> 00:08:50.118
That helps explain why sharp bodies can still have soft wingtips.

00:08:50.118 --> 00:08:54.303
Do not read the numbers as exact settings for every swallow, osprey or
heron.

00:08:54.303 --> 00:08:58.602
The calculation simplifies the wing path, and some wingbeat inputs are
estimates.

00:08:58.602 --> 00:09:01.338
Real birds change speed, angle and wing shape.

00:09:01.338 --> 00:09:05.591
The useful lesson is the scale of the problem and the importance of timing
within the wingbeat.

00:09:05.591 --> 00:09:10.895
A burst gives you several opportunities to catch an appealing posture and a
moment of lower wingtip movement.

00:09:10.895 --> 00:09:12.797
It does not guarantee a good frame.

00:09:12.797 --> 00:09:20.406
Autofocus can miss, the eye can be closed, the background can be
distracting, and a beautiful wing position may arrive between exposures.

00:09:20.406 --> 00:09:23.666
Review a short burst rather than judging a camera from one frame.

00:09:23.666 --> 00:09:27.755
Compare the eyes, the head angle, the wing positions and the detail on the
bird.

00:09:27.755 --> 00:09:32.780
If every frame is similarly blurred, investigate shutter speed, focus and
camera movement.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:38.316
If some are sharp and others are not, the changing subject motion and focus
behaviour are useful clues.

00:09:39.316 --> 00:09:41.857
Here is a simple way to review similar frames.

00:09:41.857 --> 00:09:43.614
Start with the story and posture.

00:09:43.614 --> 00:09:46.323
Which frame has the head angle or behaviour you want?

00:09:46.323 --> 00:09:49.005
Then inspect the eye and the important feather detail.

00:09:49.005 --> 00:09:53.698
Finally, check the edges of the crop for cut-off wings, branches or
distractions.

00:09:53.698 --> 00:09:55.663
Compare at the same displayed size.

00:09:55.663 --> 00:10:01.152
If one bird is shown much larger than another, it is easy to mistake that
difference for a difference in sharpness.

00:10:01.152 --> 00:10:07.367
When a burst contains nearly identical frames, narrow the choice to a few
candidates and compare them side by side.

00:10:07.367 --> 00:10:11.306
The free Burst Picker can help by ranking how crisp the detected bird looks.

00:10:11.306 --> 00:10:16.598
It runs in the browser and is a shortcut for finding candidates, not a judge
of the final photograph.

00:10:16.598 --> 00:10:22.576
A high score does not tell you whether the eye is open, the pose is
appealing, or the picture tells the story you want.

00:10:22.576 --> 00:10:27.131
Scores are relative within the set, not a universal measure of photographic
quality.

00:10:27.131 --> 00:10:30.071
For a short exercise, think of your last burst.

00:10:30.071 --> 00:10:31.990
What made you choose the frame you kept?

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:38.107
If the answer is only that it was first or easiest to see, try comparing the
top three at the same scale next time.

00:10:38.107 --> 00:10:41.915
The improvement may come from a better choice rather than a different camera
setting.

00:10:42.915 --> 00:10:49.593
Competition rules change, so this recording does not give you a current
deadline or promise that one edit is accepted everywhere.

00:10:49.593 --> 00:10:56.151
Instead, use this checklist against the organiser's rules for the exact
competition, year and category you are entering.

00:10:56.151 --> 00:10:58.615
First, find the required dimensions.

00:10:58.615 --> 00:11:02.654
Is the limit a minimum long edge, a maximum file size, or both?

00:11:02.654 --> 00:11:06.110
Are preliminary entries and shortlisted files treated differently?

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:08.320
Second, check which edits are permitted.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:14.566
Cropping, changing colour, removing objects and generating detail are
separate operations and may have different rules.

00:11:14.566 --> 00:11:17.277
Third, check what evidence you may need to retain.

00:11:17.277 --> 00:11:21.445
Keep the untouched camera original and, where useful, nearby frames.

00:11:21.445 --> 00:11:25.007
Exporting a finished crop should not be the moment you discard the source.

00:11:25.007 --> 00:11:28.621
Fourth, check the category's subject and ethical requirements.

00:11:28.621 --> 00:11:32.346
A technically suitable file can still be ineligible for other reasons.

00:11:32.346 --> 00:11:35.925
The free contest guide is a starting point with links to organisers.

00:11:35.925 --> 00:11:38.317
The organiser's current rules are the authority.

00:11:38.317 --> 00:11:44.747
If a rule is unclear, ask the organiser before entering rather than assuming
a software feature makes the edit acceptable.

00:11:44.747 --> 00:11:49.518
This approach takes a little longer once, but it protects the work you put
into the photograph.

00:11:50.518 --> 00:11:52.335
Let us use a simple example.

00:11:52.335 --> 00:11:56.396
Imagine a twenty-four megapixel original that is six thousand pixels wide.

00:11:56.396 --> 00:11:58.954
The bird is only six hundred pixels across.

00:11:58.954 --> 00:12:03.526
If you crop so the bird fills half the width, the crop is twelve hundred
pixels wide.

00:12:03.526 --> 00:12:07.158
If it fills one fifth, the crop is three thousand pixels wide.

00:12:07.158 --> 00:12:12.131
Suppose a destination requires at least three thousand pixels on the long
edge without enlargement.

00:12:12.131 --> 00:12:17.812
The tight twelve hundred pixel crop cannot meet that requirement just
because the original camera file was large.

00:12:17.812 --> 00:12:24.479
The wider three thousand pixel crop may meet the dimension requirement, but
you still need to decide whether its composition works.

00:12:24.479 --> 00:12:27.256
Take ten seconds to consider your own photograph.

00:12:27.256 --> 00:12:32.752
What is the smallest crop that keeps the composition you want and still has
enough pixels for its destination?

00:12:32.752 --> 00:12:38.775
If those goals conflict, consider a smaller print, a different image or a
wider environmental composition.

00:12:38.775 --> 00:12:48.775
Pause and consider your own photograph.

00:12:48.775 --> 00:12:51.890
The point is to make the destination part of the decision early.

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:58.089
Pixel dimensions are a constraint you can check, not something to discover
after spending an hour on the finished export.

00:12:59.089 --> 00:13:00.801
Doing this once is manageable.

00:13:00.801 --> 00:13:06.580
Repeating the same crop work across hundreds or thousands of bird
photographs is where BirdFrame is intended to help.

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:13.358
It finds birds and makes crops with Tight, Balanced or Wide framing, using
the full-size original rather than a smaller preview.

00:13:13.358 --> 00:13:15.664
BirdFrame is available for Mac and Windows.

00:13:15.664 --> 00:13:17.876
Both platforms can work with folders.

00:13:17.876 --> 00:13:20.891
On Mac, it also works with Apple Photos albums.

00:13:20.891 --> 00:13:28.566
The Photos workflow and the folder export workflow are different: Photos
edits are reversible, while folder exports create separate cropped files and

00:13:28.566 --> 00:13:30.310
leave the source files untouched.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:35.317
The two ideas to retain are no repeated cropping by hand and no downscaling
of the retained crop.

00:13:35.317 --> 00:13:38.680
Nothing is generated to fill in feathers or reconstruct a hidden bird.

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:40.989
A partly hidden bird remains partly hidden.

00:13:40.989 --> 00:13:43.502
You still review the results and choose what to keep.

00:13:43.502 --> 00:13:48.065
The example on screen shows two birds from one original and separate
exported crops.

00:13:48.065 --> 00:13:53.868
In the Mac Photos workflow, a picture with multiple detected birds can use a
combined crop containing them.

00:13:53.868 --> 00:13:57.670
Do not assume that one Photos asset becomes several separate pictures.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.466
Individual bird crops are available through export.

00:14:00.466 --> 00:14:04.370
The following recorded examples show the distinction and the practical
steps.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:10.342
First, an animation shows real detections and crops from an approved
original photograph.

00:14:10.342 --> 00:14:15.007
It demonstrates the framing choices; it is not a timed screen recording of
processing.

00:14:15.007 --> 00:14:19.566
Notice how the original stays available while different crop sizes tell
different stories.

00:14:19.566 --> 00:14:54.666
Animation of real detections and crops · not a timed processing recording

00:14:54.666 --> 00:14:58.459
Next is a sequence captured from the actual Mac app and Apple Photos.

00:14:58.459 --> 00:15:04.645
It shows choosing an album, previewing a crop, applying edits, changing the
framing and restoring the originals.

00:15:04.645 --> 00:15:09.118
The sequence is edited from real screenshots rather than an uninterrupted
mouse recording.

00:15:09.118 --> 00:15:13.739
The interface was captured in September, so some labels may differ in a
newer version.

00:15:13.739 --> 00:15:58.739
Mac Photos · real screenshot sequence recorded September 2026

00:15:58.739 --> 00:16:04.501
The useful pattern is choose the source, choose the framing, review the
result and keep a route back to the original.

00:16:04.501 --> 00:16:07.883
Windows users use the folder workflow rather than Apple Photos.

00:16:07.883 --> 00:16:14.365
Start with a small set whose originals you know, so you can compare the
result comfortably before moving on to a whole trip.

00:16:15.365 --> 00:16:21.029
If you want to try BirdFrame, it is free for up to fifty photos per run,
with as many runs as you like.

00:16:21.029 --> 00:16:23.435
The one-time purchase unlocks bigger batches.

00:16:23.435 --> 00:16:24.805
There is no subscription.

00:16:24.805 --> 00:16:29.525
Your local store shows the current price and system requirements before you
install or buy.

00:16:29.525 --> 00:16:38.032
Choose a few photographs with different challenges: one obvious bird, one
small bird, a partly hidden bird and, if you have one, a picture with two

00:16:38.032 --> 00:16:38.359
birds.

00:16:38.359 --> 00:16:42.809
Try a framing choice, inspect the crops, and compare them with the
originals.

00:16:42.809 --> 00:16:45.897
That gives you a better test than choosing only an easy portrait.

00:16:45.897 --> 00:16:48.027
Mac and Windows purchases are separate.

00:16:48.027 --> 00:16:50.819
Mac member codes do not work on the Windows version.

00:16:50.819 --> 00:16:57.772
If your club already has a Mac code, use the instructions supplied with that
code; this recording does not create a new club offer.

00:16:57.772 --> 00:17:02.346
There is no need to buy anything to follow the workshop or use the linked
educational tools.

00:17:02.346 --> 00:17:07.980
The practical question is whether the result helps you review and crop your
own photographs with less repetitive work.

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:12.424
Judge it on those pictures, not on a promise of a particular number of
minutes saved.

00:17:13.424 --> 00:17:18.362
The resource links below this recording let you revisit the calculations
without memorising them.

00:17:18.362 --> 00:17:23.253
The reach calculator compares pixels on a bird for a camera, focal length
and distance.

00:17:23.253 --> 00:17:26.623
The shutter-speed tool explores motion under its stated assumptions.

00:17:26.623 --> 00:17:29.714
The crop-size guide connects pixels with prints and screens.

00:17:29.714 --> 00:17:34.623
If your difficulty is choosing from similar frames, try the Burst Picker
with a short burst.

00:17:34.623 --> 00:17:40.484
If you are preparing a competition entry, start with the contest guide and
then read the organiser's current rules.

00:17:40.484 --> 00:17:44.813
If you simply want to see a crop, the browser demo provides a quick example.

00:17:44.813 --> 00:17:49.896
Its download limits differ from the desktop app, so read the size
information shown by the tool.

00:17:49.896 --> 00:17:51.724
You do not need to use everything.

00:17:51.724 --> 00:17:55.323
Pick the tool that answers the question holding up your next photograph.

00:17:55.323 --> 00:17:59.094
A comparison is most useful when you keep the other assumptions the same.

00:17:59.094 --> 00:18:04.248
Change one input, look at the result, and decide whether it changes your
next practical step.

00:18:04.248 --> 00:18:06.355
All the links are on the workshop page.

00:18:06.355 --> 00:18:12.468
You can also download the slides and transcript if your club wants to
revisit a section without replaying the whole recording.

00:18:13.468 --> 00:18:16.551
Before your next outing, choose one habit to practise.

00:18:16.551 --> 00:18:24.340
It could be leaving more room around a flying bird, checking a burst at the
same display size, or deciding the intended print size before making a tight

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:24.618
crop.

00:18:24.618 --> 00:18:27.065
After the outing, preserve the originals.

00:18:27.065 --> 00:18:29.289
Make crops from those full-size files.

00:18:29.289 --> 00:18:33.872
Review the bird and the composition rather than accepting a score or a
detection without looking.

00:18:33.872 --> 00:18:36.482
Export a copy suited to the place you will share it.

00:18:36.482 --> 00:18:43.683
The central lesson is simple: the pixels on the bird are limited, but your
choices about framing and selection can make much better use of them.

00:18:43.683 --> 00:18:46.514
A tighter crop is not automatically a better picture.

00:18:46.514 --> 00:18:49.798
A technically sharper frame is not automatically the best story.

00:18:49.798 --> 00:18:54.494
Use the calculations to understand the limits, and your judgement to choose
the photograph.

00:18:54.494 --> 00:18:55.716
Thank you for watching.

00:18:55.716 --> 00:18:59.582
Questions can go by email to BirdFrame; no live session is needed.

00:18:59.582 --> 00:19:04.357
The free tools, Mac and Windows download links, slides and transcript are
below.

00:19:04.357 --> 00:19:09.886
For a club discussion, compare two crops of the same original and explain
what each one keeps in the story.
